You are a safety and security analyst reading a use-case description. List
the rules the text states about the AI system's own behavior, so an analyst
can check that the hazard analysis keeps every one of them.

## What counts as a stated rule

A stated rule is a sentence or clause that says what the system must do,
must not do, or may do only under a condition or limit. It governs something
the system itself does in a conversation: what its reply says or leaves out,
which tools it calls and with which arguments, and when it refuses, escalates,
confirms, or asks for information. The rule must be explicit in the text. Do
not infer rules from risks, goals, incidents, or general good practice.

Test every candidate: you must be able to restate it as "The system must ...",
"The system must not ...", or "The system may ... only ...", where "the
system" is the AI system itself and the rest describes its own conduct. If the
honest restatement needs a different subject (the company, its staff, a tool,
a backend, a regulator) or describes a fact rather than conduct, the sentence
is not a stated rule; leave it out.

Most sentences in a use case describe the deployment, its history, or its
context, and a use case often states only a few rules. Leave out, in
particular:

- facts about models, channels, integrations, launch dates, figures, and
  adoption;
- enforcement that a backend, tool, gateway, or platform performs on its own,
  such as a request that a tool rejects or state that resets on restart;
- capabilities listed without a condition or limit, such as "the system can
  look up orders".

## One entry per distinct rule

When several sentences state the same rule, for example a summary sentence and
a tool description that repeats it, return one entry and quote the most
specific sentence. When one sentence states two unrelated rules, return two
entries, each quoting its own clause.

## Fields

- `quote`: copy one contiguous span of the use-case text exactly, character for
  character, long enough to state the rule (usually one sentence or clause).
  Do not paraphrase, shorten with ellipses, join separate fragments, or fix
  wording. You may omit Markdown emphasis markers such as `**`.
- `restatement`: the rule in at most 20 words, beginning "The system must",
  "The system must not", or "The system may". Entries in any other form are
  discarded.
- `modality`: `requires` (the system must do something), `forbids` (the system
  must not do something), or `permits` (the system may do something only within
  the stated condition or limit).

Return at most 25 entries. When the text states no rule about the system's own
behavior, return an empty list.

## Output

Return one JSON object:

```json
{"rules": [{"quote": "<exact span of the text>", "restatement": "The system must ...", "modality": "requires"}]}
```
